          "secNumber": "4.2",
          "heading": "Bijections and equal cardinality",
          "content": "### Statement and formal objects\n\n**Definition.** Sets A and B are equinumerous, A\u2248B, when a bijection f:A\u2192B exists. A bijection is injective (one-to-one) and surjective (onto), not just one of these. The object under discussion is not a mere metaphor: its elements, domain, equality condition, and allowable constructions determine what can be deduced. In particular, a statement with a universal quantifier must be justified for every member of its stated domain. A statement of existence requires an actual object or a valid existence theorem. Wherever notation abbreviates a more detailed membership claim, unfold it before applying a familiar algebraic rule.\n\n### Fully worked example\n\n**Worked case.** f:Z\u2192Z, f(n)=n+1, is a bijection even though it shifts every integer. For N={0,1,...}, g(n)=2n bijects N with the proper subset of even naturals. To check this example, first record exactly the input set or structure. Then perform each step under the definition rather than under a visual intuition. If the result is a set, an elementwise membership test is the primary certificate; if it is a map, check both uniqueness and totality; if it is a formal theorem, identify the inference used. The example shows the stated calculation in this setting. It does not, by itself, justify replacing finite inputs with arbitrary infinite ones.\n\n**A tempting error.** Concluding two infinite sets have unequal size because one is a strict subset of the other. Diagnose it by writing the disputed expression and the legal definition next to each other. Test a small counterexample when one exists; otherwise state the missing justification. A successful correction is not simply a more forceful verbal explanation. It should alter a quantified claim, a domain declaration, an equality condition, or a proof step that has been shown to be wrong. If the same error can survive the revised formulation, the repair is not complete.\n\n### Three graded exercises and answer guidance\n\n**Exercise 1 \u2014 derive.** Build an explicit bijection between N and the positive odd integers, and write its inverse. **Worked answer or proof outline:** f(n)=2n+1, with inverse f\u207b\u00b9(2n+1)=n on the odd positives. Directly test injectivity and surjectivity. Check each intermediate membership or mapping fact. If more than one construction is possible, explain what property the chosen one has that matters for the problem. A good answer can be verified by somebody who does not know the intended conclusion in advance: the definition and the visible steps suffice.\n\n**Exercise 2 \u2014 find a countercase.** Analyze the claim implicit in the mistake: **Concluding two infinite sets have unequal size because one is a strict subset of the other.** Construct the smallest useful finite case or supply an axiom-level diagnosis. **Answer check:** the proposed failure must share the same domain and hypotheses as the original claim. A picture with omitted cases is not a counterexample. When no counterexample is appropriate because the result is itself a theorem, explain why the attempted move is invalid without asserting a fabricated example. Describe which condition is essential to the valid statement.\n\n**Exercise 3 \u2014 transfer.** Apply the definition to the unit project: Two repositories enumerate student submissions differently; ask whether they have equally many identifiers, whether every identifier is processed, and whether missing indices can be repaired. Start by assigning mathematical types to the represented objects, then state whether the operation being requested preserves those types. **Answer check:** identify one ordinary input and one edge case, such as an empty relation, duplicate identifier, missing output, undefined ambient set or a new ordinal stage. If a conclusion depends on a model of real-world data, state that dependency; do not silently turn a record-management decision into a set-theoretic fact.",
